From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7921: do not bypass the DMA scheduler while the SoC firmware starts

A restart of the WF part of the MT6897 gets through the ROM commands and
sees the firmware running, but the WFDMA doesn't read the command after
that. Ten tries later the reset work gives up, and Wi-Fi stays down until
the module is reloaded.

A restart goes through mt792x_wpdma_reset(), whose mt792x_dma_disable()
sets the bypass of the DMA scheduler and leaves it set. The probe calls
that too, but with force, and the reset of the scheduler that comes with
force clears the bit again. So the firmware starts with the bit clear after
a probe and with it set after a restart. The status register of the
scheduler shows what that does:

                          after a probe     after a restart
  before the ROM commands
    bypass                0                 1
    free pages            0                 0x60
  firmware running
    free pages            0x3e8             0

With no free pages, turning the scheduler on stops the next command for
good. This also fits what was tried earlier: a reset of the WFDMA in the
restart made no difference, as mt792x_wpdma_reset() ran after it and set
the bit again.

Clear the bit before the ROM commands, on either way up. Once the firmware
runs, a restart then reads the same as a probe in all the registers of the
scheduler, free pages included, and the commands get through. Why the
scheduler ends up without pages is not known, only that this bit makes the
difference. The vendor driver sets this bit only for USB, with the
scheduler turned off in its configuration, so on this bus it never has it
on.

The clear in mt7921_soc_dmashdl_init() has nothing left to do and goes
away.

Seen on the device, with a scan after each: resets through debugfs, three
in a row, one with the interface down, one during a scan and one after a
reload of the module, then one from a command that timed out and one from a
firmware that stopped answering. A reset while connected has not been
tried.
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.c b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.c
@@ -695,7 +695,6 @@
 	/* Groups 0, 1, 2 and 15, and an ACK from HIF every 4 */
 	mt76_wr(dev, MT_DMASHDL_OPTIONAL, 0x00048007);
 
-	mt76_clear(dev, MT_DMASHDL_SW_CONTROL, MT_DMASHDL_DMASHDL_BYPASS);
 	mt76_set(dev, MT_WFDMA0_GLO_CFG_EXT0, MT_WFDMA0_CSR_TX_DMASHDL_ENABLE);
 }
 
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc_mcu.c b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc_mcu.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc_mcu.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc_mcu.c
@@ -619,6 +619,14 @@
 	mt7921_soc_read_manifest(dev, manifest, sizeof(manifest));
 	dev_info(dev->mt76.dev, "firmware in memory: %s\n", manifest);
 
+	/*
+	 * With the bypass on while the firmware starts, the DMA scheduler is
+	 * left without a free page and holds back every command once it is on.
+	 * mt792x_dma_disable() sets it, and only in the probe does a reset of
+	 * the scheduler clear it again.
+	 */
+	mt76_clear(dev, MT_DMASHDL_SW_CONTROL, MT_DMASHDL_DMASHDL_BYPASS);
+
 	ret = mt7921_soc_log_time_sync(dev);
 	if (ret)
 		dev_warn(dev->mt76.dev, "time sync failed: %d\n", ret);
